{"name":"Seal Pup","hex":"#65869b","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"seal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pinniped (Pinnipedia), particularly an earless seal (true seal) or eared seal."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hunt seals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seal"},{"term":"pup","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Pulabu."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A young dog, wolf, fox, seal, bat or shark, or the young of certain other animals.","A young, inexperienced person."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pup"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pinniped","blocks":[{"text":"Pinnipeds, commonly known as seals, are widely distributed and diverse carnivorous, fin-footed, semiaquatic, mostly marine mammals, belonging to the clade Pinnipedia. They comprise the extant families Odobenidae, Otariidae, and Phocidae, with 34 extant species and more than 50 extinct species described from fossils. While seals were historically thought to have descended from two ancestral lines, molecular evidence supports them as a monophyletic group. Pinnipeds belong to the suborder Caniformia of the order Carnivora; their closest living relatives are musteloids, having diverged about 50 million years ago."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Pinnipeds_vertical_version_montage.jpg/330px-Pinnipeds_vertical_version_montage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pinniped"},{"title":"Seal brown","blocks":[{"text":"Seal brown is a rich dark brown color, resembling the color of the dyed fur from the fur seal."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seal_brown"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Pinniped_range.jpg/500px-Pinniped_range.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wik